import AxQM.Concrete.PauliNormalizerFirstPair

/-!
# Concrete: pivot-wire decoupling of a Pauli normalizer (Figure 10.9, part 2)

This file continues the formalisation of
Nielsen & Chuang **Theorem 10.6** (the converse — the normalizer `N(Gₙ)` is generated by
Hadamard/phase/CNOT gates, Exercise 10.40) toward the multi-qubit inductive synthesis (parts 2–3,
Figure 10.9).

After the first-wire reduction (`IsPauliNormalizer.exists_cliffordCircuit_fixFirstPair`), the
composite `M := B U` is again a Pauli normalizer that **fixes the first-wire generators**
`X₀` and `Z₀` exactly under conjugation. The inductive step now needs to *decouple* wire `0`: to
recurse onto the last `n` wires, one must know that `M` maps every generator supported away from
wire `0` back to a Pauli again trivial on wire `0`. This file proves exactly that decoupling.

The mechanism is the symplectic (commutation) structure of Exercise 10.33. A Pauli string `a` that
is the identity on wire `0` commutes with both `X₀` and `Z₀`; conjugation by a unitary preserves
commutation (`unitaryConj_commute_iff`); and `M` fixes `X₀, Z₀`; so the (signed) image
`M P_a M† = z • P_b` again commutes with `X₀` and `Z₀`. Finally, the only single-qubit Pauli
commuting with both `X` and `Z` is the identity, so `b` is the identity letter on wire `0`.

Everything is about raw matrix / index data (`Matrix`, `Concrete.pauliString`,
`Concrete.xGenPauli`/`zGenPauli`, `Concrete.IsPauliNormalizer`).

## What is proved

* `pauliString_index_eq_zero_of_commute_gen` — **a Pauli string commuting with both `X_c` and `Z_c`
  is the identity on wire `c`.** The single-qubit fact that only `I` commutes with both `X` and `Z`,
  read off the symplectic criterion (Exercise 10.33): commuting with `X_c` forces `b c ∉ {Y, Z}` and
  commuting with `Z_c` forces `b c ∉ {X, Y}`, leaving `b c = I`.

* `IsPauliNormalizer.conj_apply_zero_eq_zero` — **wire-`0` decoupling.** A Pauli normalizer `M` that
  fixes `X₀` and `Z₀` under conjugation sends every Pauli string trivial on wire `0` (`a 0 = 0`) to
  a signed Pauli string `z • P_b` (`z = ±1`) that is again trivial on wire `0` (`b 0 = 0`). This is
  Figure 10.9, part 2: after `M` fixes the first-wire generators, the remaining generators
  `Xᵢ, Zᵢ` (`i ≠ 0`) — each identity on wire `0` — are mapped to Paulis supported on the last `n`
  wires, so `M`'s action restricts to an `n`-qubit normalizer on the sub-register `{1, …, n}` (the
  inductive hypothesis of `isPauliNormalizer_eq_smul_cliffordCircuit`).

* `IsPauliNormalizer.exists_conj_xGenPauli_supportedOffZero` /
  `…_exists_conj_zGenPauli_supportedOffZero` — **the generator specialisation.** The decoupling read
  directly off the remaining generators: for `i ≠ 0`, `M Xᵢ M†` and `M Zᵢ M†` are `±1` multiples of
  Pauli strings supported on the last `n` wires (`b 0 = 0`). This is the exact form the multi-qubit
  inductive step consumes as an `n`-qubit normalizer's generator action.
-/

open Matrix

noncomputable section

namespace AxQM.Concrete

variable {n : ℕ}

/-- **Only the identity commutes with both `X_c` and `Z_c`.** A Pauli string index `b` whose Pauli
`pauliString b` commutes with both standard generators `X_c` and `Z_c` has the identity letter on
wire `c` (`b c = 0`). Reading the symplectic commutation criterion (`pauliString_commute_iff`,
Exercise 10.33): commuting with `X_c` means `Even (pauliAnticommCount b (xGenPauli c))`, i.e.
`b c ∉ {Y, Z}` (`odd_pauliAnticommCount_xGenPauli_iff`); commuting with `Z_c` means `b c ∉ {X, Y}`
(`odd_pauliAnticommCount_zGenPauli_iff`); together these leave only `b c = I`. -/
theorem pauliString_index_eq_zero_of_commute_gen (b : Fin n → Fin 4) (c : Fin n)
    (hx : Commute (pauliString b) (pauliString (xGenPauli c)))
    (hz : Commute (pauliString b) (pauliString (zGenPauli c))) : b c = 0 := by
  rw [pauliString_commute_iff] at hx hz
  have hxx : ¬ (b c = 2 ∨ b c = 3) := fun h =>
    (Nat.not_even_iff_odd.mpr ((odd_pauliAnticommCount_xGenPauli_iff b c).mpr h)) hx
  have hzz : ¬ (b c = 1 ∨ b c = 2) := fun h =>
    (Nat.not_even_iff_odd.mpr ((odd_pauliAnticommCount_zGenPauli_iff b c).mpr h)) hz
  omega

/-- **Pivot-wire decoupling (Nielsen & Chuang Exercise 10.40, Figure 10.9, part 2).** Let `M` be an
`(n+1)`-qubit Pauli normalizer that **fixes the first-wire generators** `X₀` and `Z₀` under
conjugation (the output of the first-wire reduction). Then `M` maps every Pauli string trivial on
wire `0` to a signed Pauli again trivial on wire `0`: for `a` with `a 0 = 0` there are an index `b`
and a sign `z ∈ {1, -1}` with `M P_a M† = z • P_b` and `b 0 = 0`.

Since `a` is the identity on wire `0`, it commutes with both `X₀` and `Z₀`
(`pauliAnticommCount_xGenPauli`/`…_zGenPauli` vanish at `a 0 = I`). Conjugation by the unitary `M`
preserves commutation (`unitaryConj_commute_iff`) and `M` fixes `X₀, Z₀`, so the image `z • P_b`
commutes with `X₀` and `Z₀`; stripping the nonzero sign (`Commute.smul_left_iff₀`), `P_b` does too,
whence `b 0 = 0` (`pauliString_index_eq_zero_of_commute_gen`).

Applied to the generators `Xᵢ, Zᵢ` (`i ≠ 0`, each identity on wire `0`), this forces their images
onto the last `n` wires — the decoupling that lets the multi-qubit induction recurse onto the
sub-register `{1, …, n}`. -/
theorem IsPauliNormalizer.conj_apply_zero_eq_zero
    {M : Matrix (Fin (n + 1) → Fin 2) (Fin (n + 1) → Fin 2) ℂ} (hM : IsPauliNormalizer M)
    (hX : M * pauliString (xGenPauli 0) * Mᴴ = pauliString (xGenPauli 0))
    (hZ : M * pauliString (zGenPauli 0) * Mᴴ = pauliString (zGenPauli 0))
    {a : Fin (n + 1) → Fin 4} (ha : a 0 = 0) :
    ∃ b, ∃ z : ℂ, (z = 1 ∨ z = -1) ∧ M * pauliString a * Mᴴ = z • pauliString b ∧ b 0 = 0 := by
  obtain ⟨b, z, hz, hconj⟩ := hM.exists_sign_conj_pauliString a
  refine ⟨b, z, hz, hconj, ?_⟩
  have hz0 : z ≠ 0 := by rcases hz with h | h <;> rw [h] <;> norm_num
  -- transport commutation with a fixed generator through conjugation by `M`.
  have transport : ∀ g : Fin (n + 1) → Fin 4,
      M * pauliString g * Mᴴ = pauliString g →
      Commute (pauliString a) (pauliString g) → Commute (pauliString b) (pauliString g) := by
    intro g hg hag
    have h := (unitaryConj_commute_iff hM.1 (pauliString a) (pauliString g)).mpr hag
    rw [hconj, hg] at h
    exact (Commute.smul_left_iff₀ hz0).mp h
  -- `a` is the identity letter on wire `0`, hence commutes with `X₀` and `Z₀`.
  refine pauliString_index_eq_zero_of_commute_gen b 0 (transport _ hX ?_) (transport _ hZ ?_)
  · rw [pauliString_commute_iff, pauliAnticommCount_xGenPauli, ha]; decide
  · rw [pauliString_commute_iff, pauliAnticommCount_zGenPauli, ha]; decide

/-- **Decoupling of a remaining `X` generator.** For a normalizer `M` fixing `X₀, Z₀` under
conjugation and a wire `i ≠ 0`, the image `M Xᵢ M† = z • P_b` (`z = ±1`) has `b` supported off wire
`0` (`b 0 = 0`) — i.e. `Xᵢ` maps to a Pauli string on the last `n` wires. The generator
specialisation of `conj_apply_zero_eq_zero` (via `xGenPauli i 0 = 0` for `i ≠ 0`), the form the
inductive step of Exercise 10.40 consumes. -/
theorem IsPauliNormalizer.exists_conj_xGenPauli_supportedOffZero
    {M : Matrix (Fin (n + 1) → Fin 2) (Fin (n + 1) → Fin 2) ℂ} (hM : IsPauliNormalizer M)
    (hX : M * pauliString (xGenPauli 0) * Mᴴ = pauliString (xGenPauli 0))
    (hZ : M * pauliString (zGenPauli 0) * Mᴴ = pauliString (zGenPauli 0))
    {i : Fin (n + 1)} (hi : i ≠ 0) :
    ∃ b, ∃ z : ℂ, (z = 1 ∨ z = -1) ∧
      M * pauliString (xGenPauli i) * Mᴴ = z • pauliString b ∧ b 0 = 0 :=
  hM.conj_apply_zero_eq_zero hX hZ (by rw [xGenPauli_apply, ite_eq_right hi.symm])

/-- **Decoupling of a remaining `Z` generator.** The `Z`-basis mirror of
`exists_conj_xGenPauli_supportedOffZero`: for a normalizer `M` fixing `X₀, Z₀` and a wire `i ≠ 0`,
the image `M Zᵢ M† = z • P_b` (`z = ±1`) has `b 0 = 0`. -/
theorem IsPauliNormalizer.exists_conj_zGenPauli_supportedOffZero
    {M : Matrix (Fin (n + 1) → Fin 2) (Fin (n + 1) → Fin 2) ℂ} (hM : IsPauliNormalizer M)
    (hX : M * pauliString (xGenPauli 0) * Mᴴ = pauliString (xGenPauli 0))
    (hZ : M * pauliString (zGenPauli 0) * Mᴴ = pauliString (zGenPauli 0))
    {i : Fin (n + 1)} (hi : i ≠ 0) :
    ∃ b, ∃ z : ℂ, (z = 1 ∨ z = -1) ∧
      M * pauliString (zGenPauli i) * Mᴴ = z • pauliString b ∧ b 0 = 0 :=
  hM.conj_apply_zero_eq_zero hX hZ (by rw [zGenPauli_apply, ite_eq_right hi.symm])

end AxQM.Concrete

end
